course: biology
lesson: 199
title: Cat body language and cat myths: milk, nine lives and landing on their feet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cats are quieter than dogs, but they talk all the time with their tails, ears, eyes and paws, and once you learn the signs you can read a cat's mood across the room.
explain: A cat that trots in with its tail straight up like a flagpole is saying a happy hello; the same cat swishing its tail hard from side to side is saying «enough, I'm cross».
explain: Cats also carry a lot of myths: the cartoon saucer of milk, the nine lives and the unlucky black cat are stories, not science.
explain: Some legends hide a real fact: cats really do twist in the air to land on their feet, but they can still be badly hurt in a fall.
fact: A cat with its tail held straight up is friendly and greeting you, the way kittens greet their mother.
fact: A tail swishing or lashing quickly from side to side means the cat is annoyed or very excited; if it does this while being stroked, stop stroking.
fact: A slow blink from a cat is a friendly signal, and if you blink slowly back, many cats blink again.
fact: A cat lying belly up is often relaxed and trusting, but it does not always want a tummy rub; touching the tummy can set off a grab-and-kick reaction.
fact: Kneading, pushing the paws in and out on a blanket like making dough, starts with kittens feeding from their mother, and it also leaves scent from glands in the paws.
fact: Cats have a righting reflex: when they fall they twist head first, then the body, to land on their feet, but falls from balconies and high windows can still badly hurt them.
fact: Most adult cats cannot digest (break down in the tummy) cow's milk well and it can upset their tummies, so water is the best drink for a cat, despite the cartoon saucer of milk.
fact: Cats have only one life; nine lives is a saying, and in some countries the saying is seven or six lives.
fact: A black cat crossing the road brings no bad luck; in some countries black cats are even thought lucky, and in shelters black cats are sometimes adopted less often because of the myth.
fact: A cat hissing with its ears flat is frightened and warning you away, and the right thing is to leave it alone so it can calm down.
fact: When a cat turns its bottom towards your face it is being friendly; cats sniff each other there to say hello, so it is the cat version of a handshake.
exercise: A cat walks in with its tail straight up. What does it mean? | It is friendly and greeting you | It is angry and about to attack | It is frightened | It is ill
exercise: Who first greets with a tail straight up? | Kittens greeting their mother | Old cats greeting dogs in the street | Cats greeting mice in the garden | Cats greeting the rain in spring
exercise: A cat swishes its tail quickly from side to side while you stroke it. What should you do? | Stop stroking, it is annoyed | Stroke faster, it is happy | Pull its tail gently | Pick it up and hug it
exercise: A cat blinks slowly at you. What is it saying? | I feel friendly and safe with you | I am about to attack you, so keep away | I am going blind and cannot see you | I am very hungry and want my dinner now
exercise: A cat shows its tummy, then grabs your hand when you rub it. Why? | A belly-up cat does not always want a tummy rub | It wants to play with your watch because it is shiny | It is too hot | It has a tummy ache
exercise: A cat kneads a blanket with its paws. Where did this start? | When kittens feed from their mother | When cats dig holes in the garden | When cats make bread | When cats fight over a place to sleep
exercise: What else does kneading do? | Leaves scent from glands in the paws | Sharpens the teeth | Warms the room | Cleans the blanket of fleas and dust and old hairs
exercise: A cat falls from a shelf and lands on its feet. What is this called? | The righting reflex | The nine lives trick | The soft-paw cat bounce | The whirling tail spin
exercise: Can a cat still be hurt falling from a balcony? | Yes, cats can be badly hurt in falls | No, cats always land safely on their feet | No, cats never fall off anything at all | Only black cats get hurt, because they have used up their lives
exercise: A cartoon cat drinks a bowl of milk. What is the best drink for a real cat? | Water | Cow's milk | Fizzy drinks | Orange juice
exercise: Why should most adult cats not drink cow's milk? | They cannot digest it well and it upsets their tummies | It makes their fur white | It makes them too strong for their size | It is too cold for them, and cats only like warm drinks from a saucer
exercise: Someone says cats have nine lives. What is true? | Cats have one life; nine lives is just a saying | Cats really do have nine lives, one for every bad fall | Cats have nine lives only if black | Cats have seven lives everywhere
exercise: A black cat crosses the road. What does it bring? | Nothing, it is just a cat crossing the road | Seven years of bad luck for whoever saw it cross | Rain the next day | A broken mirror and a lost key by the end of the week
exercise: Why are black cats sometimes adopted less often from shelters? | Because of the bad-luck myth | Because they are less friendly | Because they are ill more often | Because they eat more food than other cats
exercise: A cat hisses with its ears flat. How does it feel? | Frightened, and it is warning you away | Happy and playful | Sleepy | Hungry
exercise: What should you do when a cat hisses at you? | Leave it alone to calm down | Pick it up to comfort it | Hiss back at it | Chase it to tire it out
exercise: Your cat turns its bottom towards your face. What does it mean? | It is being friendly, like a cat handshake | It is very angry with you and wants you to go away | It wants to be smacked | It is ill
test: «cats always land on their feet so it's fine if she jumps off the balcony» What is right? | No, cats can still be badly hurt, so windows and balconies need screens | Yes, cats never get hurt | Yes, if she has lives left, the fall only takes one of her nine away | No, cats never land on their feet
test: In Tom and Jerry Tom gets a saucer of milk as a treat. What should a real cat get? | Fresh water to drink, as milk upsets most adult cats | A saucer of milk every day, like in the cartoon, as a special treat | Milk and cheese | A bucket of cream
test: «Ginger's lost about 3 lives already lol, 6 left» What is the truth? | Cats have only one life, so he needs care like any pet | He has six lives left for sure, so he can take a few more risks | He has eight left, he is wrong | Cats get a new life every birthday
test: «black cat walked in front of my bike, I'm doomed today» What is right? | No, it is only a myth, the cat brings no bad luck | Yes, seven years of bad luck, starting from the moment it crossed | Yes, but only on Fridays | No, only white cats bring bad luck
test: «she rolled over so I rubbed her belly and she attacked me, psycho» What happened? | A belly-up cat may not want a rub and grabs on reflex | She is a wild animal and wanted to hurt you on purpose because she hates you | She wanted food | She was asleep
test: What would happen if you blinked slowly at a calm cat across the room? | Many cats would blink slowly back | It would run away screaming | It would fall asleep at once | It would hiss and attack
test: «why does she knead me like dough with her claws, it hurts» What is the reason? | It starts from kittenhood feeding and leaves scent; a thick blanket helps | She wants to bake | She is angry with you | She is sharpening her claws on purpose to hurt you because you are too soft and comfy
test: «tail's whipping about but he's purring, so keep stroking?» What is wiser? | Stop, a lashing tail means he has had enough | Keep going, purring wins over the tail every time | Stroke his tail | Hug him tighter
test: Count them: four cats walk in with tails up. How many tails, and how many happy hellos? | 4 tails and 4 hellos | 4 tails and 8 hellos | 8 tails and 4 hellos | 4 tails and 16 hellos
test: Count them: two cats and Jerry the mouse. How many ears and how many legs? | 6 ears and 12 legs | 6 ears and 8 legs | 4 ears and 12 legs | 6 ears and 10 legs
test: «why'd the cat stick its bum in my face, rude» What is the real meaning? | It is a friendly cat hello | It hates you | It wants you to leave | It is ill
test: «the cat's hissing and flattening its ears, gonna pick it up for a cuddle» What should happen? | Leave it alone until it calms down | Pick it up quickly | Blow in its face | Hiss back to show who's boss
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a domestic cat which approaches with its tail held vertically? | The animal is offering a friendly greeting | The animal is preparing to attack | The animal is in a state of fear and seeks a means of escape | The animal is unwell
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the provision of cow's milk to adult cats. | Most adult cats digest it poorly, and water is the appropriate drink | Cow's milk is the most suitable daily drink | Milk is required for the growth of the whiskers | Adult cats are unable to drink water
exam: Identify the statement which accurately characterises the righting reflex of the cat. | The cat twists so as to land upon its feet, yet may nonetheless sustain serious injury | The reflex renders the cat entirely immune to all injury from falls of whatever height | The reflex is possessed only by black cats | The reflex is a consequence of the cat having nine lives
exam: It is asserted that a black cat crossing one's path portends misfortune. Ascertain the correct assessment of this assertion. | It is a superstition without any scientific foundation | It is an established scientific fact | It applies solely to Fridays | It applies only to white cats
